{"id": "6e1e1280-2b91-42e9-92d1-4ad130d8f237", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T11:33:02.179591", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "The FsLex lexer file has a parse error. The issue is that FsLex uses a specific syntax with `#` section separators and a different format. Let me check the correct FsLex syntax.\n\nFsLex uses a format like:\n```\n{\n  open Parser\n  open FSharp.Text.Lexing\n}\n(\n  [<refinement>]\n  type lexbuf = Lexing.LexingBuffer<char>\n)\nrule tokenize =\n  parse\n    | ' ' | '\\t' | '\\r' | '\\n' { tokenize lexbuf }\n    | ...\n```\n\nThe key differences from what I wrote:\n1. FsLex uses `rule name = parse` syntax, not `let tokenize lexbuf = match`\n2. It uses `parse` keyword with pipe patterns\n3. It has sections: `{...}` for header, `(...)` for implementation, `rule ... = parse` for rules\n4. The `eof` keyword in FsLex is `eof` (not `| eof ->`)\n\nLet me rewrite the Lexer.fsl with proper FsLex syntax.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "08a1cfab-26b4-4886-9153-edde9678f14d", "tool_call": {"id": "08a1cfab-26b4-4886-9153-edde9678f14d", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"The FsLex lexer file has a parse error. The issue is that FsLex uses a specific syntax with `#` section separators and a different format. Let me check the correct FsLex syntax.\\n\\nFsLex uses a format like:\\n```\\n{\\n  open Parser\\n  open FSharp.Text.Lexing\\n}\\n(\\n  [<refinement>]\\n  type lexbuf = Lexing.LexingBuffer<char>\\n)\\nrule tokenize =\\n  parse\\n    | ' ' | '\\\\t' | '\\\\r' | '\\\\n' { tokenize lexbuf }\\n    | ...\\n```\\n\\nThe key differences from what I wrote:\\n1. FsLex uses `rule name = parse` syntax, not `let tokenize lexbuf = match`\\n2. It uses `parse` keyword with pipe patterns\\n3. It has sections: `{...}` for header, `(...)` for implementation, `rule ... = parse` for rules\\n4. The `eof` keyword in FsLex is `eof` (not `| eof ->`)\\n\\nLet me rewrite the Lexer.fsl with proper FsLex syntax.\\n\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-6cd85fc3-3dca-46ed-9a4c-e8ac25b87322", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"The FsLex lexer file has a parse error. The issue is that FsLex uses a specific syntax with `#` section separators and a different format. Let me check the correct FsLex syntax.\\n\\nFsLex uses a format like:\\n```\\n{\\n  open Parser\\n  open FSharp.Text.Lexing\\n}\\n(\\n  [<refinement>]\\n  type lexbuf = Lexing.LexingBuffer<char>\\n)\\nrule tokenize =\\n  parse\\n    | ' ' | '\\\\t' | '\\\\r' | '\\\\n' { tokenize lexbuf }\\n    | ...\\n```\\n\\nThe key differences from what I wrote:\\n1. FsLex uses `rule name = parse` syntax, not `let tokenize lexbuf = match`\\n2. It uses `parse` keyword with pipe patterns\\n3. It has sections: `{...}` for header, `(...)` for implementation, `rule ... = parse` for rules\\n4. The `eof` keyword in FsLex is `eof` (not `| eof ->`)\\n\\nLet me rewrite the Lexer.fsl with proper FsLex syntax.\\n\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
