- `lexbuf.Lexeme` returns the text from the start of the current token to the current position\n- When `digitAccumulate` is called, it starts from the position after the first digit\n\nActually, I think the issue is that I need to track the start position. Let me use `lexbuf.LexStart` and `lexbuf.LexBuffer` to get the full text.\n\nActually, the simplest approach: use `lexbuf.Lexeme` which gives the text from `LexStart` to `LexCurrentPos`. When `digitAccumulate` finishes, `Lexeme` should contain all the digits.\n\nBut wait, the `_` pattern in `digitAccumulate` would consume one extra character. I need to handle this differently.\n\nLet me try using `lexbuf.LexemeString` or `lexbuf.Lexeme` and then put back the extra character... No, that's too complex.\n\nLet me try a completely different approach: use `lexbuf.Lexeme` in the action block and manually advance the lexbuf to consume all digits:\n\n```\n| '0'..'9' as c {\n    let sb = System.Text.StringBuilder()\n    let _ = sb.Append(c)\n    while true do\n      match lexbuf.NextChar with\n      | '0'..'9' as c' -> let _ = sb.Append(c')\n      | _ -> break\n    let numStr = sb.ToString()\n    INT (System.Int32.Parse numStr)\n  }\n```\n\nBut this uses `while` and `break` which might not work in FsLex action blocks.\n\nLet me try the simplest possible approach: just match a single digit and emit it, then let the parser handle multi-digit numbers by concatenating. No, that won't work because the parser expects INT tokens with values.\n\nOK, let me try yet another approach. I'll use `lexbuf.Lexeme` and a separate accumulating rule, but handle the extra character issue:\n\n```\nrule tokenize = parse\n  | ' ' | '\\t' | '\\r' | '\\n' { tokenize lexbuf }\n  | '0'..'9' { digitAccumulate lexbuf }\n  | '+' { PLUS }\n  | '-' { MINUS }\n  | '*' { STAR }\n  | '/' { SLASH }\n  | '(' { LPAREN }\n  | ')' { RPAREN }\n  | eof { EOF }\n\nand digitAccumulate lexbuf = parse\n  | '0'..'9' { digitAccumulate lexbuf }\n  | c {\n      let numStr