fferent approach. Instead of using `let` bindings, I can use helper functions defined in the header:\n\n```\n{\nopen Parser\nopen FSharp.Text.Lexing\n\nlet parseInt s =\n  match System.Int32.TryParse s with\n  | (true, n) -> INT n\n  | _ -> failwith (sprintf \"Invalid integer: %s\" s)\n}\n\nrule tokenize = parse\n  ...\n  | _ { parseInt (lexbuf.Lexeme.Substring(0, lexbuf.Lexeme.Length - 1)) }\n```\n\nThis way, the action block is a single expression (a function call).\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "60f36758-e8d9-481a-9d33-c92a711401da", "tool_call": {"id": "60f36758-e8d9-481a-9d33-c92a711401da", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"Still a parse error, now on line 25 which is past the end of the file (24 lines). This is very strange. The file has 24 lines and ends with a newline.\\n\\nWait - the error says line 25, column ?. Let me check if there's something wrong with the file encoding or if there's a hidden character.\\n\\nActually, I think the issue might be that FsLex doesn't support `let` bindings inside action blocks `{ }`. The action block expects a single F# expression, not a block of statements.\\n\\nIn F#, `let` bindings followed by an expression is a valid expression block. But FsLex's parser might not support this.\\n\\nLet me look at the FsLex source code to understand what it accepts in action blocks.\\n\\nActually, looking at the FsLex source code (FsLex.fs), the action is parsed using `FSharpExpr.Parse` or a similar mechanism. The issue might be that FsLex uses a simplified F# expression parser that doesn't support `let` bindings.\\n\\nLet me try using `let ... in ...` syntax instead:\\n\\n```\\n| _ {\\n    let lexeme = lexbuf.Lexeme\\n    let numStr = lexeme.Substring(0, lexeme.Length - 1)\\n    match System.Int32.TryParse numStr with\\n    | (true, n) -> INT n\\n    | _ -> failwith (sprintf \\\"Invalid integer: %s\\\" numStr)\\n  }\\n```\\n\\nThis should be valid F# - it's a block of `let` bindings followed by a `match` expression. The last expression is the result.\\n\\nBut wait, maybe the issue is that FsLex's parser doesn't support multi-line action blocks at all. Let me try a single-line version:\\n\\n```\\n| _ { let lexeme = lexbuf.Lexeme in let numStr = lexeme.Substring(0, lexeme.Length - 1) in match System.Int32.TryParse numStr with | (true, n) -> INT n | _ -> failwith (sprintf \\\"Invalid integer: %s\\\" numStr) }\\n```\\n\\nThat's ugly but might work.\\n\\nActually, let me try a different approach. Instead of using `let` bindings, I can use helper functions defined in the header:\\n\\n```\\n{\\nopen Parser\\nopen FSharp.Text.Lexing\\n\\nlet parseInt s =\\n  match System.Int32.TryParse s with\\n  | (true, n) -> INT n\\n  | _ -> failwith (sprintf \\\"Invalid integer: %s\\\" s)\\n}\\n\\nrule tokenize = parse\\n  ...\\n  | _ { parseInt (lexbuf.Lexeme.Substring(0, lexbuf.Lexeme.Length - 1)) }\\n```\\n\\nThis way, the action block is a single expression (a function call).\\n\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-94a412f7-0e64-4a59-98b9-6d138896a847", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"Still a parse error, now on line 25 which is past the end of the file (24 lines). This is very strange. The file has 24 lines and ends with a newline.\\n\\nWait - the error says line 25, column ?. Let me check if there's something wrong with the file encoding or if there's a hidden character.\\n\\nActually, I think the issue might be that FsLex doesn't support `let` bindings inside action blocks `{ }`. The action block expects a single F# expression, not a block of statements.\\n\\nIn F#, `let` bindings followed by an expression is a valid expression block. But FsLex's parser might not support this.\\n\\nLet me look at the FsLex source code to understand what it accepts in action blocks.\\n\\nActually, looking at the FsLex source code (FsLex.fs), the action is parsed using `FSharpExpr.Parse` or a similar mechanism. The issue might be that FsLex uses a simplified F# expression parser that doesn't support `let` bindings.\\n\\nLet me try using `let ... in ...` syntax instead:\\n\\n```\\n| _ {\\n    let lexeme = lexbuf.Lexeme\\n    let numStr = lexeme.Substring(0, lexeme.Length - 1)\\n    match System.Int32.TryParse numStr with\\n    | (true, n) -> INT n\\n    | _ -> failwith (sprintf \\\"Invalid integer: %s\\\" numStr)\\n  }\\n```\\n\\nThis should be valid F# - it's a block of `let` bindings followed by a `match` expression. The last expression is the result.\\n\\nBut wait, maybe the issue is that FsLex's parser doesn't support multi-line action blocks at all. Let me try a single-line version:\\n\\n```\\n| _ { let lexeme = lexbuf.Lexeme in let numStr = lexeme.Substring(0, lexeme.Length - 1) in match System.Int32.TryParse numStr with | (true, n) -> INT n | _ -> failwith (sprintf \\\"Invalid integer: %s\\\" numStr) }\\n```\\n\\nThat's ugly but might work.\\n\\nActually, let me try a different approach. Instead of using `let` bindings, I can use helper functions defined in the header:\\n\\n```\\n{\\nopen Parser\\nopen FSharp.Text.Lexing\\n\\nlet parseInt s =\\n  match System.Int32.TryParse s with\\n  | (true, n) -> INT n\\n  | _ -> failwith (sprintf \\\"Invalid integer: %s\\\" s)\\n}\\n\\nrule tokenize = parse\\n  ...\\n  | _ { parseInt (lexbuf.Lexeme.Substring(0, lexbuf.Lexeme.Length - 1)) }\\n```\\n\\nThis way, the action block is a single expression (a function call).\\n\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
